# Line endings are enforced by the repository, not by each developer's core.autocrlf.
#
# WHY: every blob currently stored here is LF, but only because the machine that committed it had
# core.autocrlf=input — a personal setting. A contributor without it (Windows default is
# core.autocrlf=true, and unset is common) would commit CRLF and produce a whole-file diff on an
# otherwise one-line change. 129 files in the working tree are CRLF right now for exactly that
# reason; they normalise on the next checkout.
#
# `eol=lf` (not bare `text=auto`) because .editorconfig declares `end_of_line = lf` for every file:
# with plain `text=auto` a Windows checkout gets CRLF working copies that the editor then rewrites,
# putting the two configs in a loop. The one deliberate exception is the Windows launcher below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The Windows launcher needs CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses an LF-only batch file. This intentionally
# overrides .editorconfig's blanket [*] rule — .bat is the one place the two must disagree.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Shell entry points stay LF even on a Windows checkout — CRLF breaks the shebang line.
gradlew   text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text. The KB ingestion pipeline takes .docx/.pdf and
# extracts .png/.emf; those live under gitignored _source/ today, but the guard is cheap and the
# failure mode (a mangled binary silently committed) is not.
*.jar     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.emf     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.docx    binary
*.xlsx    binary
*.zip     binary
